The '''Kuarupíba''' ({{IPA-bl|kwɑːruˈpiːbɑː}} {{respell|kwaɾupiːba|}}) are a [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiran]] ethnicity and one of the [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous peoples of Balisca]], who traditionally inhabited the [[Alpujarra]], known to them as ''Katu-yva-kora'' ({{Literal translation|valley where the people sing by the water}}). They comprise one of the single largest Indigenous groups in Balisca, as well as one of the largest populations out of any Abyalan Indigenous peoples who remain native speakers of their respective [[W:Indigenous languages of the Americas|Indigenous language]]. When ranked amongst all Indigenous languages across the [[Algarves]], the Kuarupíba list fifth after speakers of [[Aruanãyára language|Aruanãyára]], [[Tapajóara language|Tapajóara]], [[W:Quechuan languages|Kawsani]], "[[W:Nahuatl|Nahuatl]]" and [[Walldungu language|Walldungu]].

In pre-colonial times, the Kuarupíba lived in more than 50 distinct landholding groups, and did not view themselves as a single unified group. They lived by hunting, fishing, and gathering, in the typical ethnographic Alpujarran pattern. The members of these various bands interacted freely with one another. The Kuarupíba people traditionally practice the [[Tekó religion]]. 

